      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the domain of the function $$u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$$, we need to consider the restrictions on the input value (x) that would produce valid output values for the function. \n\nSince the function contains a square root in the denominator, the radicand (the expression inside the square root) must be greater than zero to avoid taking the square root of a negative number or a fraction.\n\n$$\\sqrt{x} > 0$$\n\nThe radicand can be zero or any positive value, so, we can remove the absolute value.\n\nFinally, x must be a positive real number.\n\nPutting these restrictions together, the domain of $u(x)$ is the set of all real numbers greater than zero.\n\n$$\\boxed{(0, \\infty)}$$",
